{{Life in Balisca}}
[[File:Ampurias Tower from Tistaina Canal.png|thumb|300x300px|The [[Tistaina Canal]], which seperates {{W|carfree city|carfree}} [[W:City centre|urban core]] of the [[Ampurias]] (right) and [[Expansión]] (left) [[Boroughs of Castejón|boroughs]] of the [[Castejón Metropolis]].]]
[[File:Original design of Barcelona's city blocks.svg|thumb|300x300px|[[Coruxón]]'s grid, which has been replicated in other Baliscan cities]]
[[Balisca]] is both a [[w:List of sovereign states and dependent territories by population density|very densely populated]] and a highly {{W|developed country}} in which {{W|transport}} is a key [[w:Transport economics|factor of the economy]]. Correspondingly it has a very dense and modern {{W|infrastructure}}, facilitating transport with road, rail, air and water networks. In its {{W|Global Competitiveness Report}} for 2014-2015, the {{W|World Economic Forum}} ranked the Baliscan transport infrastructure sixth in the world.

With a total road network of {{cvt|203,374|km|mi|abbr=on}} including {{cvt|10812|km|mi|abbr=on}} of [[Autopista of Balisca|motorways]], Balisca has one of the densest [[Roads in Balisca|road network]]s in the world; much denser than [[Argentina]] and [[Brazil]], though not nearly as dense as the [[United States]]. Balisca also has an advanced railway network, that connects nearly all major mainland towns and cities, as well as a comprehensive dedicated {{W|cycling infrastructure}}, featuring some {{cvt|75,000|km|mi|abbr=on}} of paths completely independent from motorized traffic. As a result of longstanding {{W|urban design}} and {{W|urban planning|planning}} policies, {{W|cyclability}} and {{W|walkability}} are prioritized in urban areas, and {{W|city centers}} throughout the country are prohibited to all non-pedestrian traffic, with the exception of {{W|public transit}} and {{w|emergency vehicles}}. The definition of "pedestrian" in Balisca has been broadened to include anyone on any human powered vehicle that is not a bicycle, as well as people operating self-propelled wheelchairs by reason of physical disability.

The [[port of Alessandria]] and the [[port of Concepción]] are two of the world's largest seaports outside East Asia, with the former being largest port of Latin America. Alessandria maintains trade connections that cross the Atlantic Ocean (westward), while Concepción typically handles incoming trade that crosses the Pacific Ocean. 

Multimodal mobility in Balisca is substantial. On the roads it has grown continuously since the 1950s and now exceeds 400 billion km travelled per year, half of which are done by car. Around 15% of all trips in the Balisca are made by car, 25% by bicycle, 20% walking, and 40% by public transport. Additionally, Baliscan airports handled at least # million passengers in 2016. Excluding air travel, Baliscans journey more than 30&nbsp;km a day on average, which takes them just over an hour.

In 2010, 4.42 billion tons of goods traffic was registered, less than 10% of which moved by sea and inland shipping, and around 10% by road transport. The remainder was mostly by rail, constituting over 2/3's of all freight movements across Balisca.
==Modal share==
{{Main|Modal share}}
[[File:Alessandria parking garage.jpg|thumb|An underground {{W|bicycle parking station}} in [[Alessandria]]'s [[Municipio IX]] borough.]]
A modal share (also called mode split, mode-share, or modal split) is the percentage of travelers using a particular type of transportation or number of trips using said type. In freight transportation, this may be measured in kilometre-tonnes (kmt). Modal share is an important component in Balisca's drive to develop sustainable transport within numerous cities and regions. In recent years, many cities have set modal share targets for balanced and sustainable transport modes, particularly 30% of active (cycling and walking) and 50% of public transport. These goals reflect a desire for a modal shift, or a change between modes, and usually encompasses an increase in the proportion of trips made using sustainable modes.
